1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,932
Your customer clicks Place Order, but checkout freezes. They retry, worried their payment failed.

2
00:00:08,532 --> 00:00:18,642
The request travels through an API gateway into a Java twenty one checkout service, then to inventory, payment, and the database.

3
00:00:19,242 --> 00:00:27,772
Inside the JVM, temporary cart objects and repeated serialization can drive a high allocation rate.

4
00:00:28,372 --> 00:00:38,508
Garbage collection reclaims unreachable objects. Some phases pause application threads, which may contribute to tail latency.

5
00:00:39,108 --> 00:00:51,048
But do not blame garbage collection from a spinner alone. Correlate request traces, garbage collection logs, CPU, and Java Flight Recorder.

6
00:00:51,648 --> 00:01:09,548
Reduce unnecessary allocations, bound caches, and compare G one with generational Z G C using real workload tests. Protect payment retries with durable idempotency. Measure customer outcomes, not just pause time.
